“The fourth finding is really about what the right pricing mechanism should be, rather than the absolute price point.
What we found is that there isn’t one dominant model in the market. Enterprise licenses are still very common, but both buyers and competitors are moving toward more flexible models that reflect actual scale or activity. We see everything from inventory and asset-based pricing, to revenue-based pricing, to AI consumption layered on top of a platform fee.
But there are two important boundaries.
First, per-seat pricing is not particularly attractive for this type of product. Buyers don’t want the economics to get worse simply because they expand access across the organization. And as agents start doing more of the work, pricing purely around human users becomes even less logical.
Second, buyers also don’t want the opposite extreme — unlimited, uncapped consumption pricing — because that creates budget uncertainty. That’s why even competitors that meter AI generally put consumption on top of a broader platform or subscription fee rather than making it the entire commercial model.
Outcome-based pricing was also interesting: buyers like the risk-sharing concept, but attribution and measurement make it difficult, and we didn’t find competitors actually using pure outcome-based pricing at scale.
So our takeaway is to test a platform fee with capped consumption — give the buyer predictable base economics, while still allowing price to scale with actual agent activity. And potentially use something tangible, like transactions or agent resolutions, as that usage denominator.”
